# Set up Trusted Publishing

Do this once, before your first release. Trusted Publishing lets the publish job upload to PyPI
with a short-lived token minted from the job's own OIDC identity, so no PyPI API token is stored
anywhere, and it is the only way PyPI accepts the PEP 740 attestations this project signs.

{%- if git_hosting_service == 'gitlab' %}

PyPI supports Trusted Publishing from **gitlab.com only**. On a self-managed GitLab instance, skip
to [Publishing with credentials instead](#publishing-with-credentials-instead).
{%- endif %}

## 1. Register the publisher on PyPI

Sign in to PyPI and open the publishing settings:

- **First release of a new project:** *Your account → Publishing* (<https://pypi.org/manage/account/publishing/>),
  and add a *pending* publisher. PyPI creates the project on the first upload. A pending
  publisher is invalidated if someone else registers the name first, so do not wait long.
- **Existing project:** *Your projects → {{ project_slug | replace('_', '-') }} → Manage → Publishing*.

Fill in the form for {% if git_hosting_service == 'github' %}GitHub Actions{% else %}GitLab CI/CD{% endif %}:

| Field | Value |
| --- | --- |
| PyPI project name | `{{ project_slug | replace('_', '-') }}` |
{%- if git_hosting_service == 'github' %}
| Owner | the GitHub user or organisation that owns the repository |
| Repository name | the repository's name |
| Workflow name | `publish.yaml` |
| Environment name | `pypi` |
{%- else %}
| Namespace | the GitLab group or user path, e.g. `acme` or `acme/platform` |
| Project name | the GitLab project's name |
| Top-level pipeline file path | `.gitlab-ci.yml` |
| Environment name | `pypi` |
{%- endif %}

The environment must match exactly. The publish job declares `environment: pypi`, and a token
minted for any other environment is refused.

## 2. Protect the environment

{%- if git_hosting_service == 'github' %}

In the repository, *Settings → Environments → New environment*, name it `pypi`, and restrict
deployment to the default branch. Do not add required reviewers: the publish job runs on every
push to `main` and exits early when the push carried no release, so a reviewer gate would hold
every push. The review that guards a release is the pull request's, and only what is merged
into `main` can reach this environment. GitHub's [environments guide](https://docs.github.com/en/actions/how-tos/deploy/configure-and-manage-deployments/manage-environments)
has the details.

## 3. Nothing to configure in the workflow

`.github/workflows/publish.yaml` already has what Trusted Publishing needs: `id-token: write` on
the publish job, and `environment: pypi`. GitHub injects `ACTIONS_ID_TOKEN_REQUEST_URL` and
`ACTIONS_ID_TOKEN_REQUEST_TOKEN`; the upload step of `release.ship` sees them and runs
`uv publish --trusted-publishing always`.
{%- else %}

In the project, *Operate → Environments → New environment*, name it `pypi`. Under *Settings →
CI/CD → Protected environments*, protect it so only the default branch's pipelines may deploy
to it. Do not add approvers: the publish job runs on every push to the default branch and exits
early when the push carried no release, so an approval gate would hold every push. The review
that guards a release is the merge request's.

## 3. Nothing to configure in the pipeline

The `publish` job in `.gitlab-ci.yml` already requests the two tokens:

```yaml
id_tokens:
  PYPI_ID_TOKEN:
    aud: pypi
  SIGSTORE_ID_TOKEN:
    aud: sigstore
```

uv reads `PYPI_ID_TOKEN` and exchanges it for a PyPI upload token; `pypi-attestations` reads
`SIGSTORE_ID_TOKEN` to sign. Both names are fixed by their audiences (`<AUDIENCE>_ID_TOKEN`), so
do not rename them. The upload step of `release.ship` sees `PYPI_ID_TOKEN` and runs
`uv publish --trusted-publishing always`.
{%- endif %}

## 4. Check it worked

Make a release ([Make your first release](../tutorials/make-your-first-release.md)). In the
publish job's log, `release.ship` prints:

```console
OIDC token present — publishing through PyPI Trusted Publishing.
```

On PyPI, the release's files list a *Provenance* section naming this repository and the publish
{% if git_hosting_service == 'github' %}workflow{% else %}pipeline{% endif %}. [Verify a release attestation](verify-a-release-attestation.md) checks it from
the command line.

If the upload fails with `invalid-publisher`, the form in step 1 does not match the job: compare
the repository, {% if git_hosting_service == 'github' %}workflow file{% else %}pipeline file path{% endif %} and environment character by character.

## Publishing with credentials instead

For an index without Trusted Publishing (a private one, or PyPI from a self-managed GitLab), set
all three of `UV_PUBLISH_URL`, `UV_PUBLISH_USERNAME` and `UV_PUBLISH_PASSWORD` as masked
{% if git_hosting_service == 'github' %}secrets on the `pypi` environment{% else %}CI/CD variables scoped to the `pypi` environment{% endif %}. For PyPI with an API token, the
username is `__token__` and the URL `https://upload.pypi.org/legacy/`.

When all three are set, the upload (`release.ship` in the pipeline, `release.publish` by hand)
uses them and passes `--no-attestations`, and `release.attest` signs nothing. PyPI accepts attestations only through Trusted Publishing, and
other indexes may reject an upload that carries them. [Publish to a private index](publish-to-a-private-index.md)
covers the rest.

